\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{Invariant dimensions count the double cosets}
\date{2026-11-21}

\p{Let #{G} be a finite group and let #{H} be a subgroup. Work
over #{\mathbb C}. Let #{E} be the space of functions on the
left cosets #{G/H}, with
##{(g\cdot f)(xH)=f(g^{-1}xH).}
Decompose #{E} into irreducible representations. For each
irreducible #{V}, the multiplicity equals the dimension of the
#{H}-invariants in #{V}. The number of double cosets
#{H\backslash G/H} equals the sum of the squares of those
invariant dimensions. The same sum runs over every irreducible
representation of #{G}, up to isomorphism.}

\proof{
\p{A function fixed by #{H} takes the same value at #{xH} and
at #{axH} for every #{a\in H}, so it is constant on double
cosets. The indicators of the double cosets are disjoint and
span the invariant functions, so the invariant dimension of
#{E} is the number of double cosets.}

\p{Since the scalar field is #{\mathbb C}, #{|G|} is invertible.
Let #{W} be a subspace stable under #{G}, and let #{\pi} be a
projection onto #{W}. The average
##{P=\frac{1}{|G|}\sum_{g\in G}\rho(g)\,\pi\,\rho(g)^{-1}}
projects onto #{W} and commutes with the action. Its kernel is
an invariant complement. Repeating the splitting on a minimal
subrepresentation writes #{E} as a direct sum of irreducible
subrepresentations.}

\p{Choose a basis of an irreducible #{V} and let
#{\langle\cdot,\cdot\rangle_0} be the standard Hermitian
product in that basis, linear in its first argument. The
average
##{\langle u,w\rangle
 =\sum_{g\in G}\langle\rho(g)u,\rho(g)w\rangle_0}
is preserved by #{G}. Each summand is positive for
#{u\ne0}, so the average is positive definite. The assignment
#{u\mapsto\langle u,w\rangle} identifies #{V} with its dual
and identifies invariant vectors with invariant forms, because
#{\langle\rho(a)^{-1}u,w\rangle=\langle u,\rho(a)w\rangle}.}

\p{Take one complex eigenvalue of a map that commutes with the
action, as in the eigenvalue step of \ref{fdip-008B}. The
eigenspace is invariant, so an endomorphism of an irreducible
is scalar. The kernel and the image of a map between
irreducible representations are invariant, so the map is zero
or an isomorphism. A map into copies of #{V} is one scalar
on each copy.}

\p{An invariant linear form #{\ell} determines
##{(\Psi_\ell v)(gH)=\ell\bigl(\rho(g)^{-1}v\bigr).}
Invariance of #{\ell} makes the value depend only on the
coset. For #{x\in G},
##{(x\cdot\Psi_\ell v)(gH)
 =\ell\bigl(\rho(g)^{-1}\rho(x)v\bigr)
 =(\Psi_\ell(\rho(x)v))(gH),}
so #{\Psi_\ell} is a map of representations. Conversely, a map
#{\Psi} of representations determines
#{\ell(v)=(\Psi v)(eH)}, and equivariance forces
#{\ell(\rho(a)v)=\ell(v)}. Evaluation at the identity coset
inverts the two constructions. The number of copies therefore
equals the dimension of the invariant forms, and hence the
dimension of the invariant vectors.}

\p{The averaging projection onto the #{H}-invariants preserves
each invariant summand, so the invariant dimension of a direct
sum is the sum of the invariant dimensions. On the copies of
one #{V} that dimension is the square of
#{\dim V^{H}}. Summing over the summands gives the number of
double cosets. If an irreducible representation does not occur
in #{E}, its invariant dimension is #{0}: a nonzero invariant
form would give a nonzero map into #{E}. The sum over every
irreducible representation is the same number.}
}
